<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">It is budget day and City investors are ex=
pecting the chancellor, John Healey, to avoid any bold spending pledges or =
tax increases in his first fiscal statement.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Investors anticipate a cautious budget fro=
m the chancellor, reasoning that he will prioritise stability in the bond m=
arket at a time when borrowing costs are close to their highest levels in a=
bout two decades.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">With memories of Liz Truss=E2=80=99s budge=
t disaster yet to fade in the City, traders will be alert to any signs that=
 Healey is wavering on the government=E2=80=99s fiscal rules =E2=80=93 but =
the mood music from the Treasury is that fiscal stability is still in vogue=
.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">One key question for the City is how much =
fiscal headroom the chancellor has chosen to maintain. That buffer =E2=80=
=93 which measures the safety margin the government enjoys to hit its fisca=
l rules =E2=80=93 will have shrunk as UK borrowing costs rose this year. Ba=
ck in March, the headroom was a comfy =C2=A323.6bn.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Economists predict it will have fallen to =
between =C2=A315bn and =C2=A320bn =E2=80=93 a figure below =C2=A315bn may s=
pook the City.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Healey has pledged to provide households w=
ith =E2=80=9Cbreathing space=E2=80=9D from the rising cost of living pressu=
res.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Treasury has announced a new measure t=
o help =E2=80=93 the government is removing standing charges from household=
 gas bills in Great Britain.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">These fixed daily fees =E2=80=93 =C2=A3108=
 a year for the typical household on average =E2=80=93 cover the costs of c=
onnecting to a supply. They are controversial because households pay them e=
ven if they only use little energy</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Healey will ask the regulator Ofgem to end=
 this =E2=80=9Cunfair and outdated practice=E2=80=9D in 2027 or as soon as =
possible afterwards.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The plan is to shift the cost of standing =
charges into the unit rate of energy instead.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Treasury says this could save 60% of a=
ccount-holding households about an average of =C2=A345 off their annual bil=
l.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">City experts are welcoming reports that He=
aley will scrap plans to slash the annual cash Isa allowance from =C2=A320,=
000 to =C2=A312,000 a year.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">According to the Financial Times, Healey h=
ad concluded that this was the wrong way to persuade people to invest more =
in stocks and shares.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The move had been announced by Healey=E2=
=80=99s predecessor, Rachel Reeves, in an attempt to <a href=3D"https://abl=
ink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgsz=
cCSneHEAXt10KoR6IBlV61UDIGHIFAECOEMPS6b1OEZujm6684-9wdA6SbTvffzGwPWmud1eTxl=
iF4uDcGA4f0Y3AqVxgNKF_lSbfF-PtXft7lhtoNkB5DLGUuhckbx4k77Bn0dYaYD6nasHaUtWCq=
jKZ_-0Ofx_sRhRr-cfXofX9Cuwkc9JOhrqbc0jz9RLbDQmi2G__j0h_sfj6hBzlZ2RPSVtxZwJM=
q7McawcxEW69zi4Xpm1ypO1HrDUhiUVD2FGJNas/4v9/QZ-FNvQQSW-IALtaOlUT5A/h30/h001=
.hVNOPahbac1i4uOgig-FRtvkqsZgG0Uzc0zXt-mA2EQ" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">enc=
ourage people to invest in UK companies</a>.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">It would have meant that from 6 April 2027=
, anyone under 65 would only have been allowed to save up to =C2=A312,000 i=
n a cash Isa. Anything above that would have to move into a stocks and shar=
es Isa.</p>
